1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
RallyRoom turns a Twitch chat room into a cooperative
rescue crew.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:07,540
One chat, one mission, three minutes to bring everyone
home.

3
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:10,520
This recording uses local simulated inputs.

4
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:14,940
The Twitch adapter is implemented, but a consenting live
channel test

5
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:15,820
is still pending.

6
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:19,580
Viewers join with a short command, then choose one of
three roles.

7
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:21,240
Scouts reveal the route.

8
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:22,940
Builders complete the crossing.

9
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:25,200
Engineers power the rescue beacon.

10
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:29,440
Each stage unlocks the next, so the team has a shared
reason to coordinate.

11
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:33,440
The controller starts the mission from a protected local
dashboard.

12
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:38,140
The overlay shows the objective, countdown, role totals
and contributions.

13
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:41,460
It keeps raw chat and individual names off screen.

14
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:43,540
Watch the route markers appear.

15
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:46,520
Then the bridge fills in and the beacon lights up.

16
00:00:46,940 --> 00:00:50,580
One accepted action per account every five seconds keeps
repeated commands

17
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:51,700
from counting twice.

18
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:55,040
Role switching lets a small crew finish every stage.

19
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:57,640
A late arrival can join immediately.

20
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:01,500
The catch -up card names the role that can help with the
current objective.

21
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:04,080
Targets stay fixed when somebody joins.

22
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:07,620
Pause freezes the timer and rejects gameplay actions.

23
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:11,140
If chat disconnects, the mission pauses and shows the
gap.

24
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:13,880
The controller resumes after reconnection.

25
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:17,100
RallyRoom cannot recover messages that Twitch never
delivered.

26
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:21,780
The server checks exact command syntax, channel
identity, participant

27
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:24,460
roles, cooldowns and duplicate event IDs.

28
00:01:24,890 --> 00:01:28,040
The browser overlay only receives aggregate state.

29
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:31,940
Twitch tokens stay on the server and the app sends no
bot replies.

30
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:36,940
OpenAI Codex built the reducer, transport, dashboard and
tests in

31
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:38,600
the recorded main build session.

32
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:42,680
76 automated checks and three browser scenarios passed.

33
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:46,820
The source, run instructions and architecture are now
public on GitHub.

34
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:51,580
This local recording demonstrates the working mission
and recovery behaviour.

35
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:55,700
Genuine Twitch gameplay and the final channel recording
remain

36
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:57,260
the next verification step.
